ngle general principle, I'm drafting it as a top-level item with IF/THEN sub-bullets: IF a span has a deficiency/deficient-type head taking a complement naming a molecular entity (enzyme, protein, gene product/symbol) and isn't itself an established disease name, THEN it shouldn't be labeled SpecificDisease.\n\nI'm also adding exceptions: exclude genotype/carrier-state modifiers entirely, and the rule doesn't apply when the phrase is a fixed, lexicalized disease name (like \"G6PD deficiency\" used as a compound label rather than a prepositional paraphrase) or when the complement is a non-molecular entity. I'll finalize this as a clean bullet list with no extra commentary, since the task only calls for the principle itself.\n\n", "type": "thinking"}, {"text": "- **Principle: Descriptive molecular-defect phrases are not disease mentions.**\n  - **IF** a candidate span is a defect-denoting head (a \"deficiency,\" \"deficient,\" or \"deficit\" noun or adjective) whose prepositional or genitive complement names a molecular entity (an enzyme, protein, complement component, gene product, or gene symbol), **THEN** do not label the span SpecificDisease. Instead, label the conventional disease name elsewhere in the passage, using only its canonical span, or leave the span untagged if no such name exists.\n  - **Carrier or genotype descriptors:** If the span contains a genotype or carrier-state modifier (for example, \"heterozygous,\" \"carrier,\" or a count of affected relatives), exclude the span entirely, regardless of its complement.\n  - **Does NOT apply:**\n    - When the defect term is part of a fixed, lexicalized disease label (modifier plus head, such as \"iron deficiency anemia\" or a standard \"X deficiency\" disorder name used as a name rather than a paraphrase). The canonical label remains SpecificDisease.\n    - When the complement is not a molecular entity, such as a nutrient, vitamin, tissue, or organism. The standard SpecificDisease rules apply.\n    - When the defect phrase is a free-standing prepositional paraphrase of the same content, the rule does apply, even if the disease name is also present in the sentence.", "type": "text"}], "diagnostics": null, "model": "claude-haiku-5-5", "role": "assistant", "stop_details": null, "stop_reason": "end_turn", "stop_sequence": null, "type": "message", "usage": {"cache_creation": {"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens": 0, "ephemeral_5m_input_tokens": 0}, "cache_creation_input_tokens": 0, "cache_read_input_tokens": 0, "inference_geo": "global", "input_tokens": 2488, "output_tokens": 2627, "output_tokens_details": {"thinking_tokens": 2157}, "service_tier": "batch", "speed": "standard"}}}